+//! `lakeletq rotate`: the operator's side of the ring an ingest connection authenticates against.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.23-secret-rotation`. `lakelet/4.10-downsampling` made this tool's contract —
+//! stdout is the answer, stderr is one sentence, the code is the failure's class, `--json` is a
+//! format and not a second command — and this module is that contract on the three things an
+//! operator does to a running deployment's key ring:
+//!
+//! * **`--add --id <ID> --secret <HEX> [--from <MILLIS>]`** writes one key file. The operator
+//!   supplies the secret, and that is a decision rather than a gap: this course's randomness is the
+//!   seeded [`crate::engine::rng`], and a key generator built on it would be the same key on every
+//!   machine. An id that is already on the ring is **refused, never replaced** — every client
+//!   holding a token signed with the old secret would be locked out, which is the failure this
+//!   whole step is about;
+//! * **`--retire <ID> [--at <MILLIS>]`** writes the key's end, and refuses to write one before
+//!   [`earliest_safe_retirement`](crate::service::keys::KeyRing::earliest_safe_retirement): the
+//!   moment a key leaves is the moment the last token it could have signed has expired, and a
+//!   retirement that goes under that strands a client mid-flight. The default `--at` is that
+//!   instant, which is what makes the safe thing the easy thing;
+//! * **`--list`** answers the ring as an operator reads it — one line per key, oldest first, with
+//!   the window, whether it is the key that signs, and the instant it may leave. That last number
+//!   is the one the other two commands are about, and it is `none` for the key nothing newer has
+//!   taken the signing over from.
+//!
+//! # What is yours here
+//!
+//! The body of [`rotate`]: reading the flags into one of the three operations, and answering each of
+//! them as the contract above says. Everything else this file needs exists already — the ring is
+//! `crate::service::keys`, the answer is [`super::Outcome`], and the classes are the constants
+//! beside it (`WRONG`, `ABSENT`, `REFUSED`, `BROKEN`) — and the `rotate` arm of the dispatcher, the
+//! `USAGE` lines and the command's table of exits belong in `src/cli/mod.rs`, where every command in
+//! this tool is named (`lakelet/4.19-backup-restore`'s `backup` is the pattern).
+//!
+//! # Two things about the files
+//!
+//! A key file is **edited, not regenerated**: the secret is key material that this process must not
+//! have to read back out of the ring to write a retirement into it, so `--retire` changes the one
+//! line it is about and leaves the rest of the file — comments, spacing, an operator's note — as
+//! it found it.
+//!
+//! And it is written **beside the file and renamed into place**, because the daemon reads this
+//! directory once per connection: a retirement written in place is a window in which a running
+//! daemon can read half a file, and half a key file is a ring it refuses to load, which refuses
+//! clients. `rename` within one directory is the one write that has no such window.
+
+use super::Outcome;
+
+/// Answer one `lakeletq rotate` call.
+///
+/// `flags` is the command line after the word `rotate` and without `--json`, which the dispatcher
+/// takes out of the line first; the answer is an [`Outcome`], so this command cannot print half an
+/// answer and then fail.
+#[must_use]
+pub fn rotate(flags: &[&str], json: bool) -> Outcome {
+    todo!("lakelet/4.23-secret-rotation: the operator's three operations on a store's key ring")
+}
