+//! The key ring: which keys this deployment has, which one signs, and when one may leave.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.23-secret-rotation`. `lakelet/4.1-ingest-protocol` decided what a payload is,
+//! `lakelet/4.6-ingest-backpressure` decided what the daemon does when it cannot keep up, and
+//! `lakelet/4.18-cardinality-cost` decided what it does with a series it will not take. None of
+//! them decided *who may connect* — and the interesting half of that question is not the check. It
+//! is what happens the day the check's key has to change, because a client that is mid-flight with
+//! a token signed by the key an operator just deleted has been locked out by a maintenance window.
+//!
+//! So this file is a **ring** rather than a key, and the rule it exists for is one sentence: the
+//! newest key signs, both keys verify while they are open, and an old key leaves only after the
+//! longest lifetime a client could still be holding. Everything else here is that sentence made
+//! checkable.
+//!
+//! # The directory is the switch
+//!
+//! The ring is `<store>/keys/`, one file per key ([`DIRECTORY`], [`key_file`]). **A ring with no
+//! key in it means authentication is off**, and that is a decision rather than a fallback: a
+//! deployment that has not turned authentication on is a deployment with no keys, not a daemon that
+//! refuses everything, and the daemon that behaves this way is the one whose harnesses from
+//! `lakelet/4.6-ingest-backpressure` and `lakelet/4.17-soak` keep working — they connect and send
+//! batches, and they have no ring.
+//!
+//! The two states are deliberately not the same state:
+//!
+//! * the directory **is not there** — no keys, authentication off. This is what a store that was
+//!   never given a ring looks like, and what `lakeletq rotate` turns off by removing the last key;
+//! * the directory **is there and holds no `*.key`** — a refusal, naming the directory. An
+//!   operator who created the ring and then misspelled a file name has said "authenticate" and
+//!   left nothing to authenticate with, and fail-open is the one answer this must not give.
+//!
+//! # A key, on disk, by hand
+//!
+//! ```text
+//! # this store's ring: one key per file, named <id>.key
+//! id=2026-10
+//! secret=9f2b0c…
+//! not_before_millis=1700000000000
+//! not_after_millis=1700006600000
+//! ```
+//!
+//! Every line is `name=value`, `#` starts a comment, and the file's name is the key's id (a file
+//! whose `id=` disagrees with its name is refused, naming both). `not_after_millis` is **absent
+//! while the key is open** and is written on the day the key is retired, which is why the file is
+//! the state and not a copy of one: an operator rotates a deployment by writing and editing files
+//! in this directory, and the daemon reads the directory per connection, so a rotation takes effect
+//! on a **running** daemon with no restart.
+//!
+//! # A token, on the wire
+//!
+//! With a ring on disk the **first payload of a connection is the token** and every later payload
+//! is a batch, exactly as before. The token is a payload inside `lakelet/4.1-ingest-protocol`'s
+//! frame — the framing does not change, and nothing in this file knows what a frame is:
+//!
+//! ```text
+//!  0..2        u16, little endian: length of the client id in bytes
+//!  then        those bytes, UTF-8
+//!  then  u16, little endian: length of the key id in bytes
+//!  then        those bytes, UTF-8
+//!  then  u64, little endian: issued_millis, milliseconds since the Unix epoch
+//!  then  u64, little endian: expires_millis
+//!  then  64 bytes: the tag, as lower-case hex
+//! ```
+//!
+//! The tag is HMAC-SHA-256 under the key's secret over **the bytes before it** — the fields as they
+//! travel, lengths and all ([`Token::signed_bytes`]) — because signing a canonical form that differs
+//! from the wire form is a second thing to get wrong (`relay/2.11-hmac-signing`'s lesson, and the
+//! reason that step added `subtle` to the relay tree). The comparison here goes through
+//! [`subtle`]'s `ConstantTimeEq`: `==` on a tag stops at the first byte that is wrong, and the time
+//! that takes is a measurement the other end gets to repeat as often as it likes.
+//!
+//! # No clock of its own
+//!
+//! Nothing in this module reads a clock, an environment variable or a file it was not handed. Every
+//! function that depends on an instant takes `now_millis`, which is what makes "what does this ring
+//! do at this instant" a question a case can answer, and what makes the rotation window arithmetic
+//! below a property of the *files* rather than of the machine the daemon happens to run on. The
+//! daemon owns the clock (`crate::service::ingest`), and it reads it once per connection.
+//!
+//! # The rotation window, and why it is the longest lifetime
+//!
+//! A key may leave only after the last token it signed has expired, and the ring can prove when
+//! that is: the last instant a key *could* have signed is the instant something newer took the
+//! signing over, and a token signed then is live until that instant plus
+//! [`Window::MAX_TOKEN_LIFETIME_MILLIS`] — the **longest** lifetime this deployment may issue, not
+//! the usual one. A bound built out of the usual lifetime is a bound that holds for every client
+//! except the one that asked for the longest token, which is the one it strands.
+//! [`KeyRing::earliest_safe_retirement`] answers that instant and `lakeletq rotate --retire`
+//! refuses to go under it — **retiring early is a refusal, not a warning**, because a key that
+//! leaves while a token it signed is live is a client locked out mid-flight.
+//!
+//! # What this file deliberately does not do
+//!
+//! It does not generate keys: the operator supplies the secret, because a generator needs
+//! randomness and this course's randomness is the seeded `crate::engine::rng` (a ring of keys that
+//! came out of a seed would be the same keys on every machine). It does not watch the directory,
+//! and it does not talk to a control channel: the ring is read per connection instead, which costs
+//! one directory read and a handful of file reads per client and buys a rotation that needs no
+//! restart. It does not revoke a key that was *stolen* — an end instant is a schedule, not a
+//! revocation list — and it does not re-encrypt or re-sign anything that was already written.
+
+//! # What is yours here
+//!
+//! Every body below, and the private helpers they will need — the hex reader, the tag, the reader
+//! that walks a payload field by field. What this file *is* is a contract: the types, the
+//! signatures, the constants and the sentences above are the interface `tests/spec_rotation.rs`
+//! and `tests/sim_rotation.rs` already speak, and the module's rules are the design. The three
+//! things that are not written down anywhere else, and that the cases judge, are: that an empty
+//! ring means authentication is off while a directory with no key in it is a refusal, that the key
+//! which signs is the newest one whose window is open, and that a key may only leave after the
+//! longest lifetime this deployment may issue has passed since something newer took the signing
+//! over.
+//!
+//! The daemon's half (`src/service/ingest.rs`) and the operator's command (`src/cli/rotate.rs`) are
+//! yours too; the dispatch arm and the `USAGE` lines for `rotate` belong in `src/cli/mod.rs`, where
+//! every command in this tool is named.
+//!
+
+use std::fmt;
+use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
+
+use crate::error::Error;
+
+/// The directory below the store that holds the ring.
+///
+/// Below the store rather than beside it, because the store directory is what a deployment is
+/// given (`service::config`'s `store.dir`), and a ring that lived somewhere else would be a second
+/// path for an operator to get wrong and a second thing a backup has to know about.
+pub const DIRECTORY: &str = "keys";
+
+/// The suffix a key file has. Only `*.key` entries are keys; anything else in the directory — a
+/// README, an editor's droppings — is not read and not a key.
+const SUFFIX: &str = "key";
+
+/// How many bytes the tag is: the HMAC-SHA-256 digest.
+const TAG_BYTES: usize = 32;
+
+/// How many bytes the tag is *as it travels*: lower-case hex, two bytes per byte of the digest.
+const TAG_HEX: usize = TAG_BYTES * 2;
+
+/// The window a ring thinks in: the longest a token may live, and what that means for a retirement.
+///
+/// A type rather than two free constants, because the two rules below are one rule seen from two
+/// sides: a token longer than this is refused at issue time (`Window::lifetime`) *and* this is the
+/// number a retirement is measured against ([`KeyRing::earliest_safe_retirement`]). A deployment
+/// that could issue a longer token than its own retirement arithmetic allows would have a bound
+/// that is not a bound.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Window;
+
+impl Window {
+    /// The longest a token this deployment issues may live, in milliseconds: one hour.
+    ///
+    /// It is the **worst case a client can hold**, and that is the only number a rotation may be
+    /// scheduled against — a bound built from the lifetime most clients ask for is a bound that
+    /// holds for every client except the one it strands. One hour is deliberately generous and
+    /// deliberately finite: a lifetime long enough to survive a bad deploy is a lifetime long
+    /// enough that a retirement which ignores it leaves a client locked out for the same hour.
+    pub const MAX_TOKEN_LIFETIME_MILLIS: u64 = 3_600_000;
+
+    /// `asked_for`, or the refusal that says why a lifetime this long is not one of this
+    /// deployment's.
+    ///
+    /// # Errors
+    ///
+    /// [`Error::Invalid`] when `asked_for` is zero (a token that expires at the instant it was
+    /// issued is a token nobody can use) or above [`Window::MAX_TOKEN_LIFETIME_MILLIS`] (the
+    /// number the rotation window is built from), naming both numbers.
+    pub fn lifetime(asked_for: u64) -> Result<u64, Error> {
+        todo!("lakelet/4.23-secret-rotation: the lifetime a token may be issued with, and the refusal that keeps it under the window a retirement is measured against");
+    }
+}
+
+/// A key's name: what the operator types, what the file is called and what a token carries.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord, Hash)]
+pub struct KeyId(String);
+
+impl KeyId {
+    /// The name, as an operator wrote it.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn new(name: impl Into<String>) -> Self {
+        Self(name.into())
+    }
+
+    /// The name, as text.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn as_str(&self) -> &str {
+        &self.0
+    }
+}
+
+impl fmt::Display for KeyId {
+    fn fmt(&self, formatter: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
+        formatter.write_str(&self.0)
+    }
+}
+
+/// One key on the ring: a name, a secret, and the window it is good in.
+///
+/// The secret is here and is never in a `Debug`, a `Display` or an error: a key that reaches a log
+/// line is a key an attacker reads out of the aggregator (`relay/2.11-hmac-signing`'s rule for its
+/// own `Secret`, which this type follows).
+pub struct Key {
+    /// The key's name.
+    id: KeyId,
+    /// The HMAC key, as bytes. Read from hex and never printed.
+    secret: Vec<u8>,
+    /// The first instant this key may sign at.
+    not_before_millis: u64,
+    /// The instant the operator retired it, or `None` while it is open.
+    not_after_millis: Option<u64>,
+}
+
+impl Key {
+    /// The key's name.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn id(&self) -> &KeyId {
+        &self.id
+    }
+
+    /// The first instant this key may sign at.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn not_before_millis(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.not_before_millis
+    }
+
+    /// The instant this key was retired at, or `None` while it is open.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn not_after_millis(&self) -> Option<u64> {
+        self.not_after_millis
+    }
+
+    /// `true` while this key may sign: no end has been written for it.
+    ///
+    /// A property of the file and not of the clock, deliberately: an operator retires a key by
+    /// writing `not_after_millis`, and "this key has been retired" is then a fact about the ring
+    /// that two machines read the same way.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn is_open(&self) -> bool {
+        self.not_after_millis.is_none()
+    }
+
+}
+
+impl fmt::Debug for Key {
+    /// The key without its secret: a `Debug` that printed one would put it in every log line,
+    /// panic message and bug report that ever mentions a key.
+    fn fmt(&self, formatter: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
+        formatter
+            .debug_struct("Key")
+            .field("id", &self.id)
+            .field("not_before_millis", &self.not_before_millis)
+            .field("not_after_millis", &self.not_after_millis)
+            .finish_non_exhaustive()
+    }
+}
+
+/// The bytes a secret written as hex means.
+///
+/// The one hex rule of this project's ring, used by the file reader and by `lakeletq rotate --add`,
+/// so that a secret an operator typed and a secret the daemon read are judged by the same code.
+/// Either case is read (an operator may type `A5`) and lower case is what gets written.
+///
+/// # Errors
+///
+/// [`Error::Invalid`] when the text is not an even number of hex digits, or is empty: a secret is
+/// key material, and "it looked close enough" is not a thing a mac can be built from.
+pub fn secret_bytes(secret_hex: &str) -> Result<Vec<u8>, Error> {
+    todo!("lakelet/4.23-secret-rotation: a secret written as hex, as bytes — the one hex rule this project's ring has");
+}
+
+/// Where a key's file lives: one file per key, named after it.
+#[must_use]
+pub fn key_file(directory: &Path, id: &KeyId) -> PathBuf {
+    directory.join(format!("{id}.{SUFFIX}"))
+}
+
